/** Siman 105 translations part C — kereti, peleti, pitchei-teshuva, turei-zahav */
export const PART_C = {
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `From me'eit l'eit, etc., and if it was kavush in a vessel from me'eit l'eit and a vessel absorbed issur — it forbids what fell into it, for it is considered as cooked; and the Acharonim wondered, for behold it is noten ta'am li'fegam — and see Peleti; and what was difficult in this in Tosafot and Rosh and what one might answer to reconcile Orach Chayyim.`,
    '1#ב': `And some are lenient if it is not a fatty issur, for then it spreads upward; and we too are expert in this — Shach.`,
    '1#ג': `And doubtful kavush — for it is a d'oraisa issur and therefore its doubt is to be stringent; but in basar b'chalav which is d'rabbanan, as we say it is a novelty that if you feed him, etc. — its doubt is permitted — and see Peleti: if kavush in brine that is enough to place on fire — also from the Torah — see there.`,
    '1#ד': `Forbidden — see Peleti: that is, when there is a doubt whether there are here two pieces, one kavush from me'eit l'eit and one in less, and we do not know which was kavush from me'eit l'eit — therefore its doubt is forbidden; but if there is a doubt whether it rests kavush from me'eit l'eit or in less — see Peleti that one may be lenient and we establish it on presumption of kashrus.`,
    '1#ה': `In vinegar — and Shach's view is that in vinegar we require me'eit l'eit as in water, and he elaborated in proof — and see Peleti that one may not be lenient even in inferior vinegar. And Magen Avraham's view is that beer vinegar is not a sharp substance, and therefore he wrote that in beer on Pesach it is not a sharp substance, only a beverage called "bersht" — and see Peleti.`,
    '1#ו': `As much as a peel; and brine must be such that it is not eaten because of its saltiness.`,
    '2#_': `L'chatchila, even something that is customarily rinsed — certainly one rinses; nevertheless one should be cautious l'chatchila.`,
    '3#א': `Only as much as a peel — see Peleti that Shach's view is even if boiling stopped; and I wrote that we require specifically that boiling did not stop, unless in a solid item or heat at the fire as on Pesach when it was roasted in fire and dripped from its gravy — then even if boiling did not stop it forbids as much as a peel.`,
    '3#ב': `All the more it is not forbidden; and Maharshal is stringent with a solid item, for it is like a kli rishon — and see Peleti, for Tosafot's view is not like mine, and we have only Rama's words that he does not distinguish.`,
    '3#ג': `We say ten — see Peleti, for Maharshal's view in a vessel the law of tattei does not apply, etc. — and see there with earthenware vessels: one should be stringent.`,
    '3#ד': `Forbidden to pour, etc. — it is not forbidden except when the upper is cold and the lower hot; but both hot — permitted — see Peleti.`,
    '5#א': `And specifically in milk issur — see Peleti, for I clarified that we say the permitted goes and imparts taste to the issur in the issur itself and not that it imparts taste to absorb; and in issur itself as well, if the issur is not completely lean — but if completely lean we do not say — see there at length.`,
    '5#ב': `That fatty substance applies to it — that is, in a d'rabbanan issur; but in a d'oraisa matter, even if completely lean — forbidden, for we are not expert — Shach.`,
    '7#א': `Basar b'chalav — and see Peleti: if the explanation is meat that was roasted with cheese; but meat that absorbed and was roasted with other things — there is no issur from itself and it does not forbid; or one might say basar b'chalav — since this alone would be permitted, it is issur from itself — and see Peleti that the main thing is like the first explanation — see there at length.`,
    '7#ב': `That touched — and in this we are expert to distinguish between lean and fatty; and blood is a lean issur — siman 87.`,
    '7#ג': `In the lower hot; but if it cooled, even a peel is not required, for adameikar does not apply, etc., since it is only absorbed issur — Shach; and Peri Chadash disagrees.`,
    '7#ד': `Even in an issur that is not fatty — and if there is fatty substance in it, whether it forbids only as much as a peel or spreads more — Shach was uncertain — requires further study.`,
    '8#_': `And if it was cooked, etc. — that all of it became nevelah, for it is dabuk issur, and the membrane does not interrupt so there should not be dabuk issur; and one need not wonder, since we too require sixty in salting — if so, why mention cooked — behold from the time of salting it was forbidden; and if it was roasted without salting — behold it is forbidden because of blood, for we estimate against all of it; and it is possible there is a doubt whether it was cooked without salting because of blood — it is a d'rabbanan doubt; but because of milk — a d'oraisa doubt, for even though dabuk issur is d'rabbanan, nevertheless the substance of the issur is d'oraisa — requires further study.`,
    '9#א': `And the membranes — meaning a separate matter: gid and tendons are d'rabbanan and a peel suffices; but a membrane from milk, whose issur is d'oraisa — even though lean, nevertheless requires netilah — siman 87.`,
    '9#ב': `That there is no milk, etc. — and if so they do not combine to nullify, and each needs sixty — and specifically in salting, for above he ruled that milk spreads.`,
    '9#ג': `If it is not known — Shach and Peri Chadash elaborated and rooted their words: since if it spread from piece to piece they would suffice to nullify the milk — if so, when it touched all of them, behold there is sixty; and if without touching, one in two nullifies; and Maharam holds chadichah na'aseh nevelah, and if so it emits and re-absorbs; but according to the Mechaber's view, who does not hold chadichah na'aseh nevelah — it is difficult; and Pri Chadash explained that all the cut pieces have sixty, but the majority does not have sixty, and since we do not know we are concerned perhaps it touched the majority and they are forbidden, and rov batel does not apply when the majority became forbidden.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#א': `Issur that fell, etc. Taz wondered on Orach Chayyim, who wrote that a vessel forbidden through kavush may not have permitted matter soaked in it from me'eit l'eit — for when is it kavush? At the end of me'eit l'eit, and already noten ta'am li'fegam; and likewise Shach above siman 69; and Peri Chadash wrote that completion of kavush is at the end of me'eit l'eit, but noten ta'am li'fegam is not until after me'eit l'eit finishes; and Magen Avraham in Orach Chayyim siman 447 preceded him — see there. But all this does not suffice, for if so it must be that the issur emerges and the permitted enters the vessel without resting a single moment — for if one waits, still impairment and kavush come together, and this is impossible to narrow so it does not delay even one moment; therefore a remedy for this question has not yet arisen. And what appeared to me — that it is far to perceive by sense a cold thing that was kavush twenty-four hours, that all twenty-four hours until the last moment it should do no action at all to absorb or emit anything, and in the last moment of twenty-four hours it should do its action to absorb and emit — this thing reason does not compel, only it emits slowly and absorbs slowly, only taste is not sensed until completion of twenty-four hours, then the action of emission and absorption of complete taste finishes; and if so, there is no longer noten ta'am li'fegam, for most taste already emitted before and taste was also absorbed before, and it awakens to absorb so it should not be impaired, since it absorbed in it anew — see there and requires further study. And after I wrote this I found likewise in responsum Chut HaMeshulash daf 100, who also wrote this reasoning — see there and in the omissions; and the matter is close to natural reality. However I do not know from where Orach Chayyim learned this, that when one says this from himself without proof from the early ones, may their light shine. And in truth one may understand in Tosafot Chullin daf 8 side 2 s.v. one who slaughters, and likewise in Avodah Zarah, and likewise in Rosh, who all inferred from gentile oil that R' Yehuda the Prince permitted — for he holds noten ta'am li'fegam is permitted — from which plain gentile vessels are not ben yomo — see there. And it is difficult: from where do we know — for the oil R' Yehuda the Prince permitted was not shelek, for below daf 38 side 2 R' Shimon and R' Shimon bar Rabbi dispute whether shelek oil is forbidden — from which the oil R' Yehuda the Prince illuminated was plain oil without shelek; and if so, from where did it absorb — and therefore it must have been kavush in it from me'eit l'eit; and the above question is difficult for him: if gentile vessels are ben yomo, nevertheless before kavush finished, time of impairment already passed, like Taz's question — and in this Peri Chadash's answer certainly does not suffice, from where we prove gentile vessels are not ben yomo — for so much we are not concerned everything happened in one moment, and this is almost not in reality, and all the more not common. However from the Gemara and Rosh it is not so much proof, for it appears they brought the view of Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam that night spoils — only to be stringent they said me'eit l'eit, as Tosafot wrote, and the stringent practice. And likewise it appears from Rosh's words, who wrote: and all the strained explanations — if we heed our teachers; but if we say me'eit l'eit, one need not all the strained explanations — and that is to be lenient he did not rely; but to be stringent he certainly holds one should not distinguish from Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam's words; and according to their view, if one removed the issur from the vessel in the middle of the night and two hours afterward they soaked permitted matter and it fell until the second night before daybreak — behold it fell from me'eit l'eit and is kemevushal, and impairment did not occur, for the entire night did not pass in completeness — and this is certain that the entire night is required, as written above siman 103 at length; and six hours from this night and six hours from the second night do not combine to carry the night to impairment — and if so, certainly forbidden, since their intent is not to be lenient against Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam, and especially Tosafot concluded like Rabbeinu Tam, only they wrote the stringent practice. However I did not see in Orach Chayyim that Taz brought so much necessity from what he wrote in general rule 2 law 8, that a vessel forbidden in kavush is permitted l'chatchila to soak in it — for one does not soak the second meat me'eit l'eit — there is no proof to say if they soaked, b'dieved it would be forbidden; one may say he speaks l'chatchila, that noten ta'am li'fegam is forbidden l'chatchila, and this even l'chatchila is permitted, since there is no way to soak it so much time, and he was not stringent even initially. And what he wrote in general rule for one who holds permitted soaked in an issur vessel ben yomo is forbidden, and soaking less than me'eit l'eit is not permitted — if so, Beit Yosef's question: how do you find it — one may say thus: his entire intent is to say the vessel forbids what fell into it from me'eit l'eit; and we say even in a kavush vessel, even though the outer side is not surrounded by water as above in salting laws; and also according to Mahariv's view, that in a copper vessel we require fire passing underneath — he teaches that kavush helps; and it exists in reality if they soaked milk in a vessel me'eit l'eit, and after milk already soaked in it twelve hours they also placed meat in the water, and at the end of twelve hours the milk was taken but the meat remained twenty-four hours — and the soaking water is not forbidden until they forbid the meat — it deals after soaking milk me'eit l'eit one added more water — and examine. And so very much; and if so, because of kavush of meat with milk it did not become forbidden, for they were not kavush together me'eit l'eit; but because of the vessel it became forbidden, kavush in it me'eit l'eit, and all of it is not noten ta'am li'fegam, for from the time of removing milk from the vessel there were only twelve hours and it was kavush in it from me'eit l'eit — see there; and Orach Chayyim did not come here to teach us noten ta'am li'fegam and nosen ta'am for Shabbos — that is known — only his entire intent is to teach us that kavush helps in a vessel as in foods — examine. And what Orach Chayyim wrote to say metal vessels are not forbidden in kavush — Taz wondered from the Tur to distinguish — it appears he holds like Mahariv, who rules like the Yerushalmi that copper does not absorb unless fire passes underneath; and if so, it is explained that through kavush it does not forbid; and see what I wrote below on this.`,
    '1#ב': `And doubtful kavush forbidden, etc. — for it is a matter of Torah from what we say: it is a novelty that if you feed him, etc.; and from this they learned the law of kavush from me'eit l'eit — from which it is d'oraisa; and Knesset HaGedolah brought in the name of an authority that is not in my hands to examine, for it is locked away — from where do we know it is d'oraisa; and I did not understand what he would say in the proof mentioned; and perhaps he would say in kavush in vinegar and brine that is only enough to place on fire — and this is possible that it is not d'oraisa. However Rambam wrote this law of kavush kemevushal plainly and did not mention at all in his book at the end of chapter 15 of the laws of forbidden foods that a tame fish kavush with a pure fish, etc., requires two hundred — this taught us the law of kavush; from this it appears he holds kavush kemevushal like Rashi in vinegar and brine — for if he held also in water and me'eit l'eit he would have written; and likewise in chapter 7 of Shevi'it he explains on that mishnah of new sumac kavush in old oil that sumac taste is absorbed more in oil — and specifically old sumac, but not new — from which in general we do not say regarding oil kavush, only sumac is different. And likewise the baraita: carobs in wine forbid, for it gives taste in it — implying other things kavush in wine do not forbid; and likewise in the laws of terumah chapter 15 halachah 9 he ruled explicitly that all terumah vegetables kavush with non-sacred vegetables are permitted except with garlic, onions, and lettuce that are sharp, as Kesef Mishneh wrote — from which in general we do not say kavush kemevushal. However in chapter 22 of the laws of Shabbos halachah 10 he wrote: one who pickles on Shabbos is forbidden, for it is like cooking — and it implies there from the Torah; therefore there is room to say Rambam distinguishes between pickling and pickling, between brine and water, against those poskim who hold in every case kavush from the Torah — that mishnah of terumot is difficult, for it teaches: all that are kavush with one another are permitted except lettuce — why do they absorb from terumah, since kavush kemevushal; and what it says sharp versus not sharp. Again I saw Tosefot Yom Tov on the mishnah challenged thus on Shmuel, who said salted is like roasted, etc., and also wrote Rambam copied "all that are kavush" — and in truth requires further study as I wrote, that he holds like Rashi and distinguishes between kavush; but against those who disagree with Rashi — it is difficult and strained to say he holds like the Yerushalmi that teaches kavush is like shelek, and he holds shelek is weaker than cooking, even though according to our Gemara shelek is more than cooked, as Tosefot Yom Tov also wrote accordingly — and one must say he holds since terumah is d'rabbanan and terumah of vegetables is also d'rabbanan, the Sages were lenient and did not forbid it except sharp things like onion and lettuce, etc. However on Mordechai it is difficult, for he challenged Rashi who does not say kavush except in brine and vinegar, from sumac of Shevi'it kavush in oil, etc. — for if so Rabbah and R' Yochanan in chapter 25 daf 111 side 2 should be difficult, that kavush is not kemevushal — that mishnah as Tosefot Yom Tov wrote in truth; and one must say as Rambam wrote, that sumac absorbs immediately from oil; and see Tosafot there s.v. salted, etc., who also distinguish for R' Yochanan in fish whose scales softened — and likewise in sumac; and if so, even for Rashi requires further study that in a place where they softened, all agree we say kavush everywhere, even in water and oil — and strained to reconcile — requires further study. Summary: kavush in vinegar and brine one may lean to be lenient in its doubt, since a complete mishnah: all that are kavush, etc., are permitted.`,
    '1#ג': `Kavush — meaning it was kavush inside and we do not know whether it was kavush a full day — forbidden. The rabbi Taz in s.k. 3 wondered at what was written from a mikveh that was presumed that its waters diminish, etc.; and on this Nekudot HaKesef and Peri Chadash replied: here the defect is before you, for the issur is inside it, unlike there where there is no defect — and they answered his claim well; but what will they say to what was written in the preceding siman, that we establish all tereifot even if the wound healed and the defect of tereifot is before you — nevertheless on presumption of kashrus, and we say "now that they deteriorated"; and Orach Chayyim himself rules thus on cheeses and wrote proof from a mikveh that was measured and found lacking — defect before us; and nevertheless, if there were not the reasoning of chaser v'ata chaser v'ata, we would not render impure retroactively; and if so, here where chaser v'ata does not apply — why establish retroactively? Orach Chayyim. All this is according to his view that all impurity is as at the time of finding, as above, even for non-sacred; but according to what I wrote above, that is all for kodshim, but for non-sacred — presumption of kashrus, and we say "now that it deteriorated" in such a case one may declare fit. And any doubtful kavush they said is forbidden — such as when there are two vessels, one had water twenty-four hours and one did not, and we are uncertain in which vessel it was — and many such cases that do not apply now that it fell; but in a doubt like this we establish on presumption of kashrus and we say "now it fell"; and in any case this law is a dispute of the poskim and it is clear that it depends on that which is above siman 81 — requires further study.`,
    '1#ד': `Or in vinegar, etc. The rabbi in Shach s.k. 2 elaborated to challenge that in vinegar we require me'eit l'eit, and he proved this law from Rosh regarding a mouse in vinegar, who wrote that we require kavush me'eit l'eit; and behold I wonder how he decided the law to be lenient — even if Rosh's view is so, behold Rashi does not hold this pickling in cold at all; and if so, even kavush of me'eit l'eit he does not hold — for all the time of me'eit l'eit we derive a day from that which if you feed him all day; and this is for Rashi proof that therefore the explanation is not from kavush; and if so, certainly kavush in brine — the measure as Rosh wrote, enough to place on fire and boil; and if so, likewise for kavush in vinegar — for in every place Rashi equates brine and vinegar regarding kavush; and Rashi who explained in the Gemara kavush in vinegar and spices — and as kavush in spices is immediate, for that is the matter of brine — so in vinegar, for one should not distinguish in Rashi; and in Pesachim daf 39 Rashi explained: not pickled in vinegar; and in daf 76 pickling in vinegar is kemevushal, and if they were pickled — behold forbidden — behold he did not blend time at all, and the plain meaning is immediately enough to boil — forbidden in vinegar immediately, even though Mordechai in Pesachim wrote Rashi does not compare in chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah vinegar to brine — that is regarding forbidding in two hundred, because its taste is not so strong — and this is proven from Rashi's wording on vinegar that fell into groats, that for pickling in it Rashi holds they are equal — and the matter is clear. And likewise clear in Rambam, who holds lettuce is a sharp item and carries pickling, as I wrote in explaining the mishnah — and all the more vinegar, for every person recognizes there is more sharpness in vinegar than in garlic and onions included in the name lettuce, as Rambam wrote; and Lechem Mishneh wrote on Rambam regarding a plain mouse that fell into beer or vinegar that forbids, and he did not write it needs to remain in it time — because he holds beer and vinegar from their sharpness absorb more — end of his words; behold even in beer he holds it is sharp; and all the more in vinegar, which is sharp — one need not me'eit l'eit to pickle it; and behold Rashi and Rambam are among the stringent. And it appears it is the Gemara in Pesachim daf 74 side 2: omtza that became thick — Tosafot explained there s.v. became thick, etc., in the name of Rivba, and likewise Rosh in chapter 3, that it deals with raw meat placed in vinegar so the blood should congeal in it — if it remains in it until it becomes thick, the blood is uprooted from it and emitted into it, and it and its limbs are forbidden, for vinegar returns and absorbs inside the meat; and Ravina holds that even if it did not become thick, its limbs are forbidden, for it is impossible there should not be traces of blood — end of his words; and if there is no power in vinegar to absorb except kavush of me'eit l'eit — from where would the meat return and become forbidden; and even though one may say vinegar has power to extract blood by nature of vinegar — from where would its power absorb in the piece until they said: if vinegar — forbidden, it too is forbidden, even without becoming thick — were it not a minority matter, the piece would also be forbidden, as Tosafot wrote; and if vinegar is not sharp, how would it absorb in the piece — and strained to distinguish between blood and other things — from where do we distinguish and be lenient; and also that Rosh wrote regarding brine that it should be placed on fire — likewise in blood; and nevertheless we derive from it for other issurim: from this it appears that even inferior wine vinegar also carries kavush — not like Magen Avraham in Orach Chayyim siman 447, who wants to distinguish between inferior wine vinegar and fine wine — for in the Gemara we say even in inferior vinegar they congeal that it came to the scabs of the fruit; and plainly we say vinegar — if it became thick, that the piece is also forbidden — from which wherever it says "came to scabs" it absorbs — and the matter is clear. And hence even if we say like Shach's view, that Rosh who wrote on that mouse that fell in vinegar requires kavush me'eit l'eit — disagrees; nevertheless to be stringent we heed other poskim; and one may also say in Rosh he distinguished between vinegar of beer and vinegar; and in truth there is some proof vinegar is sharp, for Ravina wanted to measure it in a kav, and said it is not less than terumah; and it is difficult: if you want to be stringent to rule on it like terumah that it should absorb like terumah in a kav — it suffices to be like terumah; and behold we established: all that are kavush with one another are permitted; and here too kavush would be permitted as terumah does not forbid — and therefore one must say garlic and onions even in terumah forbid, and vinegar is likewise sharp, certainly like garlic and onions — see there; and the matter is necessary. And therefore it is more plausible in Rosh that he holds like Rashi, who explained that which he asks in vinegar — perhaps the strength of vinegar prevents giving taste li'fegam, and Rosh holds two things do not work: the sharpness of vinegar to prevent impairment and to nullify impairment, and to hurry pickling so one should not need me'eit l'eit so much — we do not say; and therefore Rosh explained it was me'eit l'eit — thus it appears to me in Rosh's words; but however Rosh's words may be, in any case the clear proof mentioned decides that vinegar is like brine regarding kavush; and if so, one may say the author cited to be stringent; but above in any case there is a doubt, perhaps it does not enhance at all — if so, so much he did not want to be stringent in vinegar, for it is a double doubt, whether it enhances and whether it is kavush — so much we are not stringent; but here the plain meaning is to be stringent, we heed.`,
    '2#_': `Heat of a kli rishon, etc. See Shach, who elaborated in s.k. 5 on the laws of iruy and rishon. First Shach's view was stated, that Rabbeinu Tam's view is established stringently, that iruy cooks as much as a peel, and even if boiling stopped, nevertheless it absorbs, as proven in chapter Keitzad; and that which Rama wrote above siman 68, that when boiling stopped it is permitted to pour on the chicken — meaning it does not emit and absorb at once; and I did not understand his words, for behold Rama above in siman 92 seif 7 in the hagahah wrote for practical law, and it is from responsum of Maharam: if boiling went from the pot and boiling stopped — it is not iruy, and even a peel is not required — and this is not like Shach. And it is clear Shach intended that which we say there: hot meat into cold milk requires a peel; and likewise he challenges from dripping from gravy, etc. — behold all boiling stopped; but still it did not help: if we say that sugya that teaches one peels means not from the law of iruy, nevertheless a peel is required, for it does not cook, only absorbs — if so, from where do we further say according to Rabbeinu Tam's views that iruy cooks and nevertheless does not cook more than as much as a peel — behold we did not find mention of this; and on the proof of hot into cold it is difficult to distinguish to say it cooks and nevertheless only as much as a peel — and all the strain is from that which we establish tattei gavar, and nevertheless a peel is required, as Tosafot wrote in many places; and if we say there it is not iruy when boiling stopped — if so, from where do we have this; and as I wrote, in truth Tosafot in Zevachim, as they descended, distinguish between boiling stopped — and they establish that in Pesachim when boiling stopped they ruled that iruy cooks entirely — see there. And also according to Rabbeinu Tam and his supporters there is no distinction between absorbed and cooked, for what cooks absorbs, and what does not cook does not absorb, as appears explicitly from Tosafot in Zevachim and likewise Rashba daf 92, who brings proof from the Yerushalmi that what cooks absorbs — and he who holds all the more absorbs needed to enter strained explanations — see there; and according to Shach's views, even for Rabbeinu Tam when boiling stopped it must be that it absorbs — and this is impossible for Rabbeinu Tam, for according to him there is no distinction between absorbed and cooked; and on Shach's proof from hot into cold I already wrote above in siman 92 that one should say it deals with a solid item; and from dripping from gravy — in truth difficult for Shmuel, and for Rav in truth there is no difference between boiling stopped and not stopped — and so too must be according to Shach's views, for in the Gemara we say for Rav from dripping from gravy, etc., that it heats the earthenware and the earthenware drips to the gravy and it is roasted because of something else — it is clear that it also cooks and not only absorbs; and one must say also according to his view a difference between Rav and Shmuel. However it is difficult to me: from where do we have Maharam's distinction and to be lenient between boiling stopped and not stopped; and as I wrote, all Tosafot's strain was to say for Rabbeinu Tam iruy cooks entirely; and see there the chapter of challah and hag'alah; but according to what is established for me that iruy does not cook more than as much as a peel — and that is as Tosafot wrote from Shmuel — if so, from where are we further lenient between stopped and not stopped; therefore it arose in my mind that this is difficult for Maharam for Rav, who said ila'i gavar and cooks — if so, how do you find a kli sheni mentioned everywhere: if the vessel was cold and he put boiling water from a kli rishon hot — thus ila'i gavar and it becomes for him a kli rishon like Shmuel, who put cold into boiling from a kli rishon — tattei gavar. And strained to say: if that boiling water he put in a cold vessel and the water grew strong and the law of hot is upon them like ila'i, he again poured into a cold vessel — so much we do not say ila'i gavar, and we judge it as kli sheni — this is strained; and also difficult is the mishnah that teaches: into the bowl it is permitted to put spices — behold in the bowl one pours from a kli rishon; and for Rav, who holds ila'i gavar — behold it cooks entirely, as we say regarding dripping from gravy that it heats so much to absorb because the vessel returns and cooks it; and if so, behold here a kli rishon entirely; and therefore Maharam holds: since he holds for Rav ila'i gavar means without boiling stopped; and that which is permitted to pour into the bowl — that is when boiling stopped; if so, we find even for Rav kli sheni. Rather this question from putting into the bowl, etc., is still difficult for Tosafot in Zevachim, who hold Rav and Shmuel dispute when boiling stopped, and nevertheless Rav holds ila'i gavar — if so, the question is in its place for Rav: how does he put into the bowl and ladle; and it would appear Tosafot should say: even though Rav holds ila'i gavar, nevertheless with spices it is different, for R' Yehuda holds even in a kli rishon they are not cooked — if so, even for the rabbis it is not cooked in a kli sheni, even though ila'i gavar — see there. But Maharam does not hold this distinction to divide, and therefore he needed to distinguish between boiling stopped and not stopped; and if so, requires further study, for even for Rav there is a difference — the question from dripping from gravy, etc., is in its place: how do we say for Rav it is resolved — behold when boiling stopped Rav concedes. Therefore it appears to distinguish: there is a difference between stopped boiling — meaning in something cooked in a bowl and a vessel that interrupts between it and the fire, and the walls of the vessel preserve the heat — therefore when one pours and boiling stops, the vessel's heat from the fire ceases and it has no power to cook; but roasting at the fire itself absorbs power and heat of fire — and if so, wherever its heat is disconnected in its interior from heat of fire, it is like moving a kli rishon from place to place; and if so, there on Pesach, which is roasted, it is as if boiling did not stop, for always on the gravy roasted in fire there is power of fire; and if it separates from the meat, it is like two first pots separated from one another with power of fire in it — and this is clear and correct. And if so, also hot that fell into cold one may explain thus — heat of roasting; except if so, he should have explained, and the rabbi should not have concealed hot that fell into cold, that it deals with hot roasted — requires further study. And it is possible this is Rama's intent: one must say adameikar absorbed — a peel is required — this is a general stringency, that it deals with when boiling stopped. However Tosafot and Rosh, who challenged in Shabbos daf 2: one puts hot into cold and not cold into hot — Rashi explained because tattei gavar; and Tosafot, Rashba, and Rosh challenged: if so, difficult for Rav, who holds ila'i gavar, and likewise for Shmuel — behold a peel is required; and they entered strained explanations; and it is difficult: what is the question — perhaps it deals with boiling stopped; rather we learn that even when boiling stopped they hold they dispute; and likewise it appears from many places; and Tosafot and Ran, who wrote thus — that is, Rabbeinu Tam holds without boiling stopped it cooks entirely, as appears from their wording; but according to what is established for us that it cooks only as much as a peel — there is no proof for boiling stopped, and we did not find it for Maharam literally. And likewise one must say in Ran: he distinguishes between boiling stopped and not stopped, and nevertheless he challenged in hot into cold — Tosafot's question — and did not answer that it deals with stopped — and it is clear. And know that above many poskim hold: if it cooked in part of a vessel, hag'alah is required in the entire vessel; in iruy we do not say thus — for from the cutting board it spreads along the length of the vessel and not in the thickness of the vessel until it is forbidden only as much as a peel, or in iruy it does not spread at all — examine. And behold in siman 68 Shach brought Mahariv's view, whom Beit Yosef and Rama followed, that a copper vessel does not absorb unless fire passes underneath; and Shach wondered: behold the mishnah teaches: one who cooked in it and one who poured boiling into it requires purging and rinsing — and therefore in a copper vessel; if earthenware, breaking is required. However because of this it does not apply that one may always say it deals in every case with earthenware, and according to Rambam's views in explaining the mishnah and in his composition, that which in every case requires breaking is in a chatat; but in other kodshim, even in copper, only purging and rinsing; and this mishnah is all in every case and in other kodshim, whose law in every case is purging and rinsing; and in truth Rambam's words are wondrous, as is known, and as I wrote in many places. And the rabbi Ba'er HaZev wrote that Rambam has proof from the Yerushalmi that Semag brought in the laws of hag'alah — and I did not see proof from there as it appears. But from that Yerushalmi in the chapter HaKirah there is proof, for it says that iruy never cooks, only as much as it absorbs; and it challenges this: a baraita teaches: even in a copper vessel thus; and you can say, etc.; and it is difficult: why bring proof from a baraita that teaches even in copper thus; and likewise the first answer, that it means in every case — how was the mishnah hidden from him that plainly teaches purging and rinsing are required — and therefore in copper, if in every case breaking is required; and why challenge from a baraita — and therefore the mishnah is unnecessary; one may say it nevertheless deals in every case and purging and rinsing suffice in other kodshim, and therefore he needs the baraita. However from that baraita Shach properly cut in the Yerushalmi that even in a copper vessel purging and rinsing are required for iruy alone. But not only on Mahariv is his question, but also on Rashba, whom Shach himself brought, for there in Terumat HaDeshen daf 92 side 2 he wrote: and from Ramban's explanation we learn that even a copper vessel into which one poured, whether from a kli rishon or kli sheni — it absorbs and requires hag'alah — for they did not say in the Yerushalmi that it does not absorb except when fire passes underneath, not to require purging and rinsing when pouring consecrated boiling into it, but it absorbs and requires hag'alah — end of his words. And on this too Shach's question: behold plainly it teaches: or he poured boiling into it — purging and rinsing; and explicit in a baraita in a copper vessel; and for Ramban too the mishnah must be not like Rambam, who holds purging helps in every case, as the Yerushalmi explains on the mishnah "one," etc., that it deals with earthenware and absorbs; and it is difficult: why breaking — for Ramban, absorption without cooking does not require purging; and if so, even in every case breaking should not be required; and one must say nevertheless in every case hag'alah does not help, and therefore breaking is required — and this is not like Rambam, for whom hag'alah helps. And likewise one may understand in Pesachim daf 76 in Tosafot s.v. granted, who challenged that the spit should forbid the Pesach — and answer that a wooden spit does not absorb heat so much like earthenware and flour. And it is difficult: behold even in a copper vessel it is clear in the mishnah that iruy into it boiling requires purging — from which it receives heat that cooks; and according to Rabbeinu Tam cooking and absorption are one — if so, how would wood not cook — strained to say this is according to Rashbam, who holds iruy absorbs but does not cook; if so, one may distinguish: even though iruy has power to absorb in a vessel, but that the vessel receive heat that roasts the food — it does not cook at all, only absorbs. And with this one may resolve Maharsha's question, why Tosafot challenged Rav and not Shmuel — for according to his explanation it is resolved in the Gemara for Shmuel from the mishnah what he puts and what he adds — if Tosafot would challenge and answer the Gemara, establishing it in hot earthenware — requires further study: perhaps iruy only absorbs and does not heat, and one need not distinguish between every case and broken vessels — requires further study; but for Rav, who establishes in cold earthenware, and nevertheless we say earthenware receives heat and roasts the Pesach — if so, they properly challenged Rav: is it resolved — behold the spit forbids; to distinguish between every case and wood Tosafot did not hold at all in the question, and answer — see there. Rather this is strained — R' Yehuda is the one who answers thus, and R' Yehuda holds in the chapter HaKirah daf 42 like Rabbeinu Tam, that it cooks, and one should not distinguish at all between absorption and cooking; and therefore Maharsha did not pay attention to the answer I wrote — if so, the above question returns to its place. Therefore it appears Rabbeinu Tam explains in the Gemara that which it says on iruy into it boiling — absorption without cooking — meaning it was already cooked in the first pot and he poured into his second vessel, and cooking does not apply, only absorption; and if so, according to this the verse and mishnah teach: do not say specifically if it cooked in this vessel it requires breaking and purging, but if it already cooked and afterward came to a vessel where it did not cook again in it — behold already cooked and standing, and purging is not required — it teaches that nevertheless it is required, since the vessel absorbs, etc. And see there the Yerushalmi that specifically requires fire passing underneath — otherwise it does not absorb; and the mishnah deals with fire passing underneath; and it teaches even though it cooked before and was not asked. And one must say that which Tosafot proved in Shabbos that iruy cooks, because it teaches "he poured into it boiling" — for according to this explanation there is no proof here, for in the mishnah it deals with fire passing underneath; Tosafot's words to answer the second that Tosafot wrote here — but for the first answer, in truth there is no proof from here, only from elsewhere, as written there — many proofs — see there; and with this all questions are resolved. Further it appeared that Mahariv and Rashba hold: that which was doubtful in our Gemara is plain to the Talmud of the land, as is common in the Bavli — it should not have been resolved; and in the Yerushalmi it was resolved — we follow the Yerushalmi, and he holds that certainly when pouring into boiling copper, even though the vessel does not absorb, since fire does not pass underneath — nevertheless the meat and gravy in the vessel cook from it inward and contract and cook, as Rosh wrote in the chapter Kirah — see there. And if so, it is cooking without absorption — and this is also the question in our Talmud: if so, purging is required. And with this it is resolved: the Gemara wanted to resolve whether cooking without absorption requires purging, from that we taught "he poured into it boiling"; and Rashi explained: he holds that just as absorption without cooking requires purging, so too the reverse; and this is strained: who would balance on one scale purging for what did not absorb at all, and what is the nature of this purging if it did not absorb at all — for that which he argued purging if it did not absorb without cooking — there the language of purging properly applies to remove what was absorbed. But according to the above it is resolved: in the Gemara he holds like the Yerushalmi that copper does not absorb unless fire passes underneath; and if so, that which teaches "he poured into it boiling" — because meat and gravy cook, as written — and he properly resolved that cooking without absorption requires purging; and the Gemara rejected: perhaps like the other view, who holds copper absorbs, and it deals that fire does not pass underneath; and if so, this view in the Yerushalmi holds cooking without absorption requires purging; and if so, one may say copper does not absorb, only meat and gravy cook and contract — and it is cooking without absorption. And with this one could also resolve Lechem Mishneh's question on Rambam, who copied the mishnah "dripped from its gravy," etc., and did not explain hot earthenware and hot flour — for we establish like Shmuel; and I already wrote above on this — see there. And it appears Rambam certainly holds: if we say nevertheless a peel is required — since gravy was forbidden as much as a peel, it absorbed on Pesach as much as removal, and likewise in flour as much as a kometz; only if we say cooking without absorption requires purging — why did the mishnah not teach it, and how did the mishnah omit this great law of Zevachim — one must say that in truth it teaches for him: or he poured into it boiling, that iruy does not forbid at all, even as much as a peel; and therefore because of cooking without absorption; but for the view that it is not required — one may say iruy cooks as much as a peel, and therefore iruy is because of absorption. And if so, it is resolved: that which the Gemara challenges Shmuel: when it absorbed — nevertheless a peel is required — not a natural presumption that perhaps it immediately cools until it does not even absorb as much as a peel (and see in Magen Avraham that it is only a general stringency, and it is difficult from where we challenge that perhaps it is not a stringency) — only the question is from the mishnah "iruy into it boiling," etc., and one need not because of cooking without absorption — for if so, let it resolve, etc.; and therefore one must say there is absorption; but above the Gemara challenges the opposite: if it is proven from the mishnah that nevertheless hot as much as a peel — if so, cooking without absorption does not require purging — for otherwise why did the mishnah not teach it, as I wrote; and if the question is difficult, let it resolve that purging is not required, and the Gemara rejected in hot earthenware; but Rambam, who is uncertain whether cooking without absorption requires purging, as clear in his book — therefore ruled hot into cold one peels; and if so, here too to be stringent, even in cold earthenware and cold flour, removal is required, as I wrote — examine. And in any case we clarified there is no place for Shach's question on Mahariv for many reasons, and a question on Rashba and Tosafot — examine.`,
    '3#א': `If both are hot from a kli rishon, etc. — see Shach s.k. 8, who brought Maharshal's view of a solid item, and even rice and millet if they are in a kli sheni — nevertheless the law of kli rishon applies to them, since there is no cooling partition; and Shach did not decide; and I did not understand — if so, from where do Tosafot in Zevachim and Shabbos prove from that which teaches "he poured into it boiling" that in every iruy it cooks as much as a peel — perhaps the vessel cools what is hot that fell into it until it immediately cools and has no power to cook even as much as a peel; and that which we say regarding "he poured into it boiling" that requires purging — there it deals with pouring consecrated meat into a vessel, and it is a solid item that always preserves its heat and the vessel is not cold to cool it at all, and it is always a kli rishon — and with this it is certain that it cooks in the vessel at least as much as a peel; but in pouring a thin item that loses its heat through cooling of the vessel — perhaps it immediately loses its heat and does not cook at all — therefore Shmuel, who holds one peels, deals with meat that fell into milk and it is a solid item that does not lose its heat at all and remains always a kli rishon; and on the contrary, with this one may reconcile the precision of the mishnah that teaches: one should not put spices into a kli rishon — implying that it is permitted to pour; and in the conclusion it teaches: but one may put into the bowl — implying iruy is forbidden; and Tosafot already wrote that the inferences contradict; and according to what I wrote it is resolved — for it is not decided how he can say one should not pour on them, implying even a thin item — and this is permitted, for it does not cook; and likewise no reason to say: but one pours with rice and millet and the like, a solid item — forbidden to pour, for this cooks — therefore the tanna stated plainly; and therefore Tosafot must be — requires further study. And the main proof of Maharshal is from what the poskim wrote: if one poured boiling water and there was a fly in the bowl — the fly forbids and is not included in sixty — and from this he learned its law; and Rama rejected because of iruy forbids; and Shach in s.k. 8 appears that he does not hold — why did he cite a fly, every iruy forbids; and according to what I wrote it is resolved: boiling stopped, and were it not for the fly there would be no iruy; but a fly, since it is a solid item, even when boiling stopped — forbids; and if so, his proof goes.`,
    '3#ב': `Issur that one placed, etc. — see Shach s.k. 10, who brought Maharshal's view that since the vessel has no emission from its body — if so, tattei and ila'i do not apply, and it always forbids and all becomes forbidden; and likewise Ra'ah; and Shach challenged and argued from the sugya in Pesachim daf 76 in the sugya of dripping from gravy onto earthenware — there it implies that even in a vessel ila'i and tattei apply — see there. Heaven forbid to say the sugya of the Gemara was in its place in concealment of a matter — but one may reconcile that he certainly holds regarding absorption and emission: in a vessel, since it has no emission from its body, tattei gavar does not apply, for it is easy to emit and absorb — this is regarding absorption; but that the body of the vessel should receive heat from the thing that fell into it until its heat is like a kli rishon and it again roasts what falls in its gravy — in this there is no difference between vessel and food: what relevance has this, that it receive heat in its substance, to that which has no emission in its substance — behold we judge the body and substance of the thing whether it receives heat and preserves its heat so much, etc.; and if so, what difference is there between vessel and food — and if so, there in Pesachim the Gemara's question from dripping gravy on the oven — the oven should receive heat and be so hot that it roasts the thing — in this there is no difference between vessel and food, and he properly inferred like Rav, who holds ila'i gavar — requires further study. However, the other proofs Shach brought are clear proofs that nevertheless in an earthenware vessel there is room to be stringent — not because of Maharshal, who has no emission from its body, only as Tosafot wrote in Zevachim daf 95 side 2 s.v. he poured, who brought in the name of the Yerushalmi that he wanted to say iruy does not cook at all and it is like a kli sheni, and he wanted to bring proof from that mishnah "he poured into it" — and he rejected it, for the mishnah teaches "he poured into it boiling," etc., in earthenware, for earthenware absorbs more, even in a kli sheni; and if so, since this reasoning is mentioned in the Yerushalmi and Tosafot, and also Maharshal's view tends to be stringent — if so, from where to reject and be lenient; and from that which the Gemara challenges from dripping gravy on earthenware, etc. — there is no proof to the contrary, for one may say that even though it hurries to absorb, but that it receive heat and again roast the drop — it is obvious it equals other things, and the Gemara challenges properly; but regarding quickness of absorption one may say as I wrote. And in truth Ramban's view, as Rashba wrote in his name in Terumat HaDeshen daf 92, does not imply thus, for he inferred from that dish of salt in which they broke it — even though salting does not cook; and it is difficult: the guilt is in earthenware, and it hurries to absorb even without cooking — if so, what proof is there for other vessels; rather we learn he does not hold thus; and it is possible the question why break it — let it be salted in it a second time, for even though it hurries to absorb, to emit we did not hear — if so, behold it is permitted to salt in it, for it is not hot at all; but this is strained in his intent, and it is simple he does not hold to distinguish, only according to his view that absorption and cooking are two matters; and if so, that which the Yerushalmi answers "earthenware absorbs" — the intent is not that there is a difference between earthenware and other things, only absorption is different; and if purging and rinsing are not required, only hag'alah, as Ramban wrote there — in earthenware it does not exist in reality, purging and rinsing, for he holds like Rashi and Tosafot that earthenware has no hag'alah at all, and therefore he challenges there from copper that has purging — we learn it cooks; but according to Tosafot's explanation it properly implies that in earthenware one should be stringent; and if so, Maharshal and Ra'ah in any case are stringent with a vessel — one may say in earthenware.`,
    '3#ג': `Forbidden to pour, etc. — this is when the upper is cold and the lower hot, for steam forbids; but both cold or both hot — even if the lower is hotter — nevertheless permitted, for this law is taken from the mishnah in chapter 5 of Machshirin, as the mishnah teaches: one who pours from cold to cold and from hot to cold is pure; from cold to hot — impure; and we establish it like the first tanna, that specifically cold into hot is forbidden; and likewise Rambam in the laws of food impurity; and he gave the reason that steam rises upward and mixes it; but otherwise permitted, for there is no connecting stream in forbidding — as Maharil wrote in responsum siman 72; and Taz gave the reason because of connecting stream — and if so, even when both are cold or both hot they are forbidden, and it is not only cold into hot.`,
    '5#א': `That the milk of an animal is lean, etc. — and for this Rosh in the chapter Kol Shehu and Tur in Orach Chayyim siman 467 wrote that milk that fell into a pan does not forbid the entire pan — their intent is with lean milk — requires further study on the words of Sefer HaTerumah that Mordechai brought in chapter 3 in his name, who wrote that therefore Rashi explained on that olive-sized piece of fat that fell into a pot, which is a ladle — that if it were without gravy, even that piece on which it fell would not be forbidden, only as much as a peel — requires further study, for behold milk spreads, and in any case that piece is entirely forbidden; and Sefer HaTerumah is not available to examine, and requires further study.`,
    '5#ב': `And permitted that was roasted with it fatty, etc. — see Shach s.k. 19 on the difficulty in Tur's words, for he ruled that what became forbidden cannot forbid. And Shach answered to distinguish between the body of the issur — for it imparts taste from the permitted — but regarding what is absorbed we do not say it imparts taste; and Rama distinguishes in Darchei Moshe between lean milk — in this we say it imparts taste — and not in other matters. And Taz wondered on him in s.k. 10, for we derive this law of imparting taste from reicha miltha, that we say permitted imparts taste to issur, and there it does not deal with milk, only meat — and in truth, like his words; and that which he wrote to reject Shach's words — Shach already answered in Nekudot HaKesef; but on Shach's words one should analyze from what Tosafot wrote, for the fat of the gid spreads — and one may answer the fat of the gid is lean; and further, that fat does not forbid more than the gid; and since the gid does not forbid, its fat is not forbidden — and this is Rabbenu Yerucham's view, etc.; and likewise Rosh there; and it is difficult: what of it if the fat were entirely permitted and they did not treat it as issur at all — nevertheless there is fat here and it imparts taste to the gid to spread through the entire thigh. And for this answer we do not say the fat of the gid is lean; and likewise Rosh wrote in the chapter Kol Shehu regarding a grain found in a chicken on Pesach, that it forbids the entire chicken because of rolling — the wheel carries taste through all of it; but if it did not roll, it forbids only as much as removal; and he does not distinguish whether the chicken was fatty or this proves he does not hold it imparts taste even to the body of the issur; and likewise Magen Avraham inferred in Orach Chayyim siman 447; therefore one must say the answer in truth is to distinguish between something that by nature is sometimes fatty or lean, such as meat — in this we say it imparts taste, like that case of scent; but the gid in its substance is lean and fatty substance cannot apply to it; and likewise chametz on Pesach, which is lean by nature — in this we do not say the permitted goes, etc.; and all this is in the body of the issur; but in absorbed issur we do not say thus regarding fatty substance — and Shach answered; and therefore Tur wrote two pieces of meat in which issur was absorbed and permitted roasted together, that if the issur is lean, even if the permitted is fatty — nevertheless it does not forbid more than as much as a peel, since the issur is absorbed, the fatty substance does not impart taste to carry, etc. — and this is Shach's answer; and even though meat sometimes has fatty substance — both answers are necessary; and this law we learn: if so, what lacks a peel should forbid again — and so always. And according to the authors who hold what became forbidden cannot forbid, etc. — still difficult: meat that has fatty substance forbidden by a peel should forbid one another; and if so, still difficult: a thigh that has a gid, since there is fat of the gid — according to this view that fatty meat forbidden as much as a peel from the gid should forbid from here onward; and likewise a chicken from a grain; and therefore one must say Shach's answer as well — both are necessary — and this is clear; and otherwise Rashba's view, as Peri Chadash wrote explicitly, that we do not say at all the permitted imparts taste to issur — therefore one may be lenient — and both answers as I wrote are true. That which Maggid Mishneh wrote in the laws of Pesach and Shulchan Aruch copied in Orach Chayyim siman 447 seif 1: a loaf of chametz that touched a loaf of matzah and there is nothing there that spreads — it forbids only as much as a peel — implies there is fatty substance that spreads to forbid all of it, even though chametz in itself is lean — nevertheless permitted imparts taste to it; and likewise Magen Avraham inferred there — possibly he nevertheless disagrees; and Shulchan Aruch who copied it followed his view, for here he omitted this law of what became forbidden cannot forbid, etc., and was concerned to be stringent. But in truth his view is void against Tosafot, Rosh, Tur, Rashba that I wrote; and especially one may say in what Maggid Mishneh wrote "and there is nothing there that spreads" — his intent is not on fatty substance, only the explanation that they are in a pot — for even though there is no gravy, then steam in the pot spreads taste through all of it, for he holds possibly like Ran, and he cited a decided matter; and therefore for practical law the main thing is as I wrote.`,
    '7#_': `Or basar b'chalav — Shach in s.k. 15 elaborated whether the explanation is meat that absorbed milk and that meat was roasted with something else, or meat that was roasted with milk — and he agreed to be stringent. And behold in what he wrote in the question from the Gemara without stirring and covering — behold it did not emit; and it is difficult: behold it absorbed from milk and became issur in its own right; and he properly answered on this Beit Yosef's answer, that it does not emit in the permitted piece, only as much as a peel or removal, but every piece of the cutting board should be forbidden. And Shach wrote this is correct. And I did not understand: behold that piece that fell on a drop of milk — for Rashi it rests in the gravy but not all of it; and if so, the piece beneath it is entirely in the gravy; and for Rashi it is entirely outside the gravy, but the piece beneath it is half in gravy; and for R' Yehuda it is as if all is in the gravy — if so, since that first piece is like issur from itself and absorbs in the second piece, behold there is gravy that carries absorption inside and in the entire piece; and until here we say removal — meaning in roasting; but this piece resting in gravy — it is obvious: if it receives absorption and taste that carries, it is as if it fell inside the gravy; and likewise for Ran, who holds in a drop of milk the taste is because of steam of the pot — this too in steam of the pot spreads through the entire piece; and therefore Ran wrote explicitly, and likewise Magen Avraham, that the entire piece is forbidden; and Beit Yosef's words were not hidden from them, that roasting forbids only as much as removal — for here there is no roasting — and it is clear. And there is no answer here, only as Ran and Rashba wrote; and nevertheless after he concluded chadichah na'aseh nevelah — in truth even without stirring and covering at all, all is forbidden for R' Yehuda; and this is strained, for the Gemara's language implies that which it says "apparently he holds it can be squeezed out — forbidden" refers to that establishment where he did not stir at the beginning, only at the end; but he did not return to the first establishment, that it deals without stirring at all — why should the Gemara bring this establishment at all, that he did not stir at the beginning, only at the end — do we not know that if it is not chadichah na'aseh nevelah, it is not applicable to forbid all because of its kind, and is milk a kind of meat — it should have said immediately: behold it did not absorb and did not emit — apparently he holds it can be squeezed out — forbidden; and everything is resolved — and why did the Gemara need this establishment, that it deals without stirring at the beginning, only at the end — and the matter is very strained; and also according to the law, that we say R' Yehuda forbids even with stirring at the beginning, etc., and the rabbis permit even if he did not stir at the beginning, only at the end — if so, it appears R' Yehuda in every case permits, if R' Yehuda also deals as Ran wrote without stirring at all — and all are equal for issur; if so, the sages who disagree in every case and said it is not forbidden until it gives taste in the gravy, etc. — their words have two explanations, and they fit his understanding: in the case where he did not stir at all — if so, what of it — in the entire pot there is sixty — nevertheless that piece remains in issur, for according to the rabbis it is absorbed issur and does not leave that piece at all, and other pieces do not help to permit it, and issur is not squeezed from it at all; and if so, one must say "until it gives taste," etc., means to forbid another — but that piece is forbidden; whereas if he stirred and covered at the end — for the sages even that piece is permitted, for it was squeezed and permitted; and if so, how can one say two explanations in the sages' words — but according to the disputants it is resolved, for it does not deal at all with one who did not stir at all — that is not in their dispute; and for all opinions it does not forbid the pot — it only deals with covering at the beginning or at the end; and therefore for the sages in every case even the piece is permitted — see there. And therefore Maggid Mishneh wrote well: in every case, even in basar b'chalav, it is not called issur from itself primarily, for the language is strained for him. And also according to Shach and his supporters, who call it from itself — I truly did not understand what difference there is between chadichah na'aseh nevelah in basar b'chalav and other issurim in general regarding a piece fit to be served to guests — there is properly a difference, for there one is lashed for eating meat, and behold here is the body of issur itself, unlike permitted absorbed from issur, for one is not lashed — if so, there is no permitted body of issur here; but regarding this, that it should forbid another piece — in any case, if we say that piece is chadichah na'aseh nevelah, even if Elijah came and said the issur was entirely squeezed out — nevertheless forbidden — if so, it is obvious that piece forbids, and "what became forbidden cannot forbid" does not apply — what of it that issur does not go — the body of the piece goes, as Ran wrote explicitly; if so, in all issurim likewise, as Ran wrote; and if we say that when it is clear to us it was squeezed, it returns to be permitted — if so, since issur can spread, what of it that permitted spreads — behold it was squeezed — if so, in basar b'chalav too we should say thus, and say a difference that in basar b'chalav, even when it is clear the milk was squeezed, nevertheless the meat is forbidden, unlike other issurim — when it is clear the issur was squeezed, it returns to be permitted — if so, see there. But this is strained, for we do not find in any posek (except in responsum of Maharil that I wrote above in his name siman 94) to distinguish between basar b'chalav and other issurim for the view that in all issurim chadichah na'aseh nevelah — if so, there is no proof from Tur and Shulchan Aruch whether they hold that in basar b'chalav they forbid — meaning it is not proven to them to say this view that what became forbidden cannot forbid; and Tosafot's question is not difficult — if so, the peel should forbid again as much as a peel, for he holds like R' Eliezer, that we do not say in other issurim chadichah na'aseh nevelah, and in basar b'chalav a peel does not apply, as Ran wrote, and also its peel is other issurim and not chadichah na'aseh nevelah for R' Eliezer — if so, see there; but according to what is established for us chadichah na'aseh nevelah in all issurim, and difficult from salting and a peel returns and forbids as much as a peel — therefore one must say what became forbidden cannot forbid, etc. — if so, in basar b'chalav too one may say thus, that from the cutting board one should distinguish. And it is possible nevertheless to be stringent, for we hold like Rabbeinu Tam to be stringent, but not to be lenient, lest the halachah is not like Rabbeinu Tam, only like R' Eliezer — if so, this view that what became forbidden cannot forbid goes; and one may say that since it became nevelah, it forbids like Ran's view (and even though it implies above siman 22 like Ra'avan that we do not say on half a piece chadichah na'aseh nevelah — if so, there is no proof from here that a peel returns and forbids as much as a peel — nevertheless from salting it is difficult, as the explicit authors wrote) — except this is strained, for otherwise that which absorbs, etc., is difficult for them, as above — as I wrote. And behold at first glance I would say these words are necessary in Tosafot in Pesachim daf 75 "it was cut," etc., who proved specifically from what is roasted — for we say regarding five that became mixed, etc., that a kohen comes while eating breast and thigh — how can he do so — behold breast and thigh forbidden to non-priests — impossible to roast it with the Pesach and one must cut it, and it is not roasted — except b'dieved one need not roasted — see there. And the matter needs study: behold Pesach is roasted on fire, and in roasting it is clear in the Gemara that it forbids only as much as a peel or removal — if so, what is the question to take a lean kid that does not spread and roast, and breast and thigh with as much as removal give to the kohen numbered with them, and the remainder permitted to non-priests without hesitation. And therefore I would say at first glance he holds like Rabbeinu Efraim — if so, one may say what became forbidden through as much as removal can forbid up to as much as removal; and so always, if we say chadichah na'aseh nevelah — only we do not say chadichah na'aseh nevelah except in basar b'chalav, as above — with this it does not apply, as I wrote. And behold Rashba in Terumat HaDeshen wrote: therefore only in basar b'chalav chadichah na'aseh nevelah — one who eats half a kezayit of milk and half a kezayit of meat is lashed — unlike other issurim, where permitted does not combine with issur, only in kodshim; and likewise Mordechai — if so, it implies in kodshim we hold permitted combines with issur, and even R' Eliezer concedes chadichah na'aseh nevelah. And with this the question is resolved properly, that removal forbidden from breast and thigh returns and forbids as much as removal, and so always, until the entire piece is forbidden; and if so, this is Riva's view, that it should forbid the entire Pesach for non-priests — removal forbidden from breast and thigh returns and forbids, for permitted combines with issur, and so always — thus it arose in his mind at first glance. However from that which we taught in Zevachim daf 97: a wafer that touched a wafer — remove its place; and the Gemara there: until it absorbs in its thickness — see there; Rashi wrote: permitted combines with issur — and we say it forbids only the place it absorbed — difficult: behold every piece and the wafer should be forbidden, as above; and strained to say this comes according to the view that we do not say chadichah na'aseh nevelah — and likewise in kodshim; and Riva's question: why does the Gemara later challenge, why does he need a remedy to bring a kohen, etc. — what is the question, perhaps he holds like the halachah of chadichah na'aseh nevelah — this is strained, and one must say even for Rabbeinu Efraim the main reason in basar b'chalav is not because permitted combines with issur, only since this alone would be permitted, as Tosafot wrote — and this does not apply in kodshim — see there; and if so, Riva's wonder stands in its place — requires further study. However from everyone's words who wrote that therefore a peel does not return to forbid as much as a peel, that chadichah na'aseh nevelah does not occur, or absorbed issur does not emerge — I did not understand the mishnah: if it touched the earthenware of the oven — scrape its place — for it deals with a hot oven, as Tosafot wrote there s.v. we taught, etc. — if so, it roasts in its heat the peel of the Pesach, as explained there in the Gemara; and if so, behold full issur and full nevelah — why should it not forbid a second time as much as a peel, for behold it is full nevelah; and Tosafot there answered: therefore a peel suffices and not removal, for it is a little gravy — but the above question is not resolved, that a peel should return and forbid as much as a peel, and there would be requiring twice as much as a peel — strained to say that which teaches "one shall scrape" means besides the peel of contact — this is strained — requires further study.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Called kavush. Ba'er Heitev — and see in responsum Makom Shmuel siman 52 and in responsa in Orach Chayyim siman 447 s.k. 16.`,
    '2#_': `With rinsing suffices. See in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 1 siman 56, who wrote regarding milk that fell into a jug of olive oil and they sensed it after an hour or two and there was not sixty in the oil against the milk — that it is seemingly simple to permit, for cold into cold does not absorb; however Tosafot's view in Avodah Zarah daf 35 is that olive oil absorbs and emits even when cold because it is strong and sharp; and even though one may reject their words, nevertheless one may not be lenient against Tosafot's view unless in a place of need or great loss — see there; and see in the book Be'er Yaakov in siman 103, who elaborated greatly on this and raised to permit even without great loss and without time of need; and one sage disagrees with him, and his view is in a place of great loss — requires further study whether one may permit; and nevertheless he wrote that specifically in the case of Shevut Yaakov where an impure thing fell into cold olive oil; but if olive oil remained in an issur vessel from me'eit l'eit (it appears there is a textual error and it should read less than me'eit l'eit) — one may permit — see there his reason; and he, may his light shine, replied to him to uphold his words that even in the case of Shevut Yaakov it is simple to permit even without a slight loss of "a bit in the pot" — see there.`,
    '3#_': `And doubtful kavush. Ba'er Heitev — and see in Knesset HaGedolah that his view is like Taz, that specifically in a doubt where there are here two pieces, one kavush from me'eit l'eit and one in less, and we do not know which was kavush from me'eit l'eit — therefore its doubt is forbidden; but if there is a doubt whether it rests kavush from me'eit l'eit or less — one may be lenient, for we establish it on presumption of kashrus; and see in responsum Chavot Yair siman 17, who challenged him that the main thing is like Rama's view — see there; and see what I wrote above siman 96 s.k. 1. And see in responsum Pri Tevuah siman 25 regarding a well of water that a gentile dug and a corpse fell into the water and died, and there is a doubt whether it was kavush in it from me'eit l'eit — and he leaned to be lenient, since there is here only a doubt of kavush and there is here only a prohibition of a positive commandment, and also there is room to doubt perhaps it is noten ta'am li'fegam — see there; and see in responsum Noda B'Yehuda part 4 siman 26, who raised that kavush kemevushal does not apply at all to all things, but only those specified in mishnayot of Shevi'it and terumot, and likewise meat whose nature is to give taste in kavush; but there are things whose nature is not to absorb in kavush, and therefore those species that are not specified as forbidden in kavush — one may say perhaps their nature is not to absorb in kavush — there is room to be lenient in doubtful kavush — see there at length.`,
    '4#_': `[ Aside from meat with milk. Peri Chadash wrote: however to cook them is forbidden, for against cooking it is a d'oraisa doubt — see there; and likewise explained in Peri Megadim above siman 64 in Meshivtzot end s.k. 1 and in the introduction to the laws of Bach, and below siman 111 in Sha'ar HaDatz s.k. 3; and likewise explained from what I wrote above siman 87 s.k. 3 — see there. However in responsum Minchat Ani siman 35 he agreed to the ruling of the rabbi who ruled there to permit l'chatchila to cook in doubtful kavush, where the doubt is whether it was kavush from me'eit l'eit — and that is according to Taz's words, who permitted also in other issurim because of initial presumption, and also Peri Chadash and Nekudot HaKesef did not challenge him on the essence of the law, only on the proof he brought from the mikveh, and he elaborated on this and concludes that also Chavot Da'at who forbids in cooking means in certain kavush and the doubt is in two pieces, where it is not applicable to say establish on presumption; but in one piece and doubt of me'eit l'eit — permitted even in cooking a positive commandment — requires further study. ]`,
    '5#_': `In vinegar. Ba'er Heitev in the name of Shach who disagrees; and I, Azriel, in responsum Panim Me'irot part 1 siman 64 also disagrees — see there; and I wonder, for he himself in part 2 end siman 39 brought proof to the contrary — see there.`,
    '6#_': `A kli rishon in which yad soledes bo. See Shach s.k. 5 who disagrees with Maharshal, who is stringent even when the yad does not soledet bo; and see in responsum Bach siman 20, who ruled like Maharshal's view because the Yerushalmi implies accordingly — for if like Shach's explanation in the Yerushalmi's words it is difficult, for from the law it is so and not because of distance; and the author of Shach himself conceded to him — see there; and see in responsum Teshuvat Chen siman 6, who elaborated on this and also raised for the law to be stringent like Rashal without great loss; but when not standing at fire — one need not be stringent at all — see there.`,
    '7#_': `In which yad soledes bo. See in the book Bechor Shor in his novellae on Chullin daf 103, who wrote that since we are not expert in the measure of an infant's gullet being burned, therefore in any case in a d'oraisa matter it is fitting to be stringent with a kli rishon until the doubt leaves his heart that there is not the heat mentioned; and even in d'rabbanan one needs investigation on this — and not like the world's custom to estimate at a time when one cannot hold the hand because of heat — it is called yad soledes bo only when it exists — see there.`,
    '8#_': `One should be cautious with a matter l'chatchila. And see in responsum Teshuvat Chen from siman 1 through siman 16 on the laws of kli rishon, kli sheni, and iruy at length. [ Language of the author of Halachot Gedolot, may his light shine, in the chapter Gid HaNasheh, Zolkva edition daf 79 side 2: "And where one poured milk into a cup — it is clear to him in cold water and it is fine to eat meat from it. Why? For milk, even at the time they milk it, is cold." And likewise the end: "that one threw curdled milk (meaning: cottage cheese) into a cup; and even though cottage cheese is salty, for we say salted is like roasted where it is not eaten because of its saltiness — but if it is eaten because of its saltiness — not," end of his words. From a manuscript Shulchan Aruch of the rabbi, the gaon, our teacher Rabbi Tzvi Hirsch Cohen, may his light shine, of Frankfurt and Vilna. ] (8) A hot issur fell. See in the book Chemdat Daniel siman 1, who wrote that it is not forbidden immediately if one removed it immediately, unless it remained a while, as written in siman 92 seif 2: if one stirred and covered immediately — permitted — see there; and what difference to me whether one stirred and covered or removed the issur — see there.`,
    '9#_': `From the heat of a kli rishon. [ Ba'er Heitev — and see what I wrote above siman 94 s.k. 7 from this. ]`,
    '10#_': `That it is outside the gravy. See what I wrote above siman 92 s.k. 1 in the name of Noda B'Yehuda.`,
    '11#_': `Requires netilat makom. See in responsum Shemen Rokeach part 2 siman 15, who raised that if one cooked it thus without netilat makom and there is not sixty against the netilah — it is permitted (on this see siman 91 in Ba'er Heitev s.k. 1); however specifically in something that appears fatty; but in something that appears lean, netilah by law is forbidden without sixty against the netilah — see there; and likewise wrote Peri Megadim in Meshivtzot s.k. 11.`,
    '12#_': `If they were roasted together. Shach s.k. 19 — and see in responsum Tiferet Tzvi part Orach Chayyim siman 22 in the middle of the responsum.`,
    '13#_': `But a vessel. Ba'er Heitev in the name of Shach: however in fatty issur absorbed in a vessel, etc. — and see in Magen Avraham siman 458 s.k. 37, who ruled explicitly that even oil entirely absorbed in a vessel does not forbid all of it; and see in responsum Noda B'Yehuda part 4 siman 44, who wrote that one should be lenient like Magen Avraham's view — we do not abandon what is clear to Magen Avraham because of Shach's doubt — see there.`,
    '14#_': `But not in fatty issur. [ See what I wrote siman 91 s.k. 2 and siman 95 s.k. 6. And see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 93, who wrote: There was an incident with a woman who salted six geese for a wedding feast, and the manner of placing them on a slanted board as is the way of salting was thus: two on two and two on top of them, and they did not touch one another, neither on their sides nor did the upper touch the lower; afterward they found in the innards of one of them a disqualifying wound, and there was great loss and need of a wedding feast; and I said: if we had known that the tereifah was placed on the bottom of the board, there would be no doubt at all about the upper ones, only about the one placed at its side, for brine also flows from the side; and if so that one was nullified one in one, since the issur is only because of absorption there is no chatichah hare'uyah lehischabed; but there is a doubt perhaps it was from the upper ones and brine flowed on all the others. And behold even if so there is here only a d'rabbanan issur of chatichah hare'uyah lehischabed; and if so, since it is close to certainty that there is sixty in the geese against the brine that flowed from one goose — if we estimate by approximation, even though we hold to estimate against all of it — nevertheless it appears to me one should distinguish, for certainly in cooking, even though we now see how much it is, nevertheless we may say it melts from itself and absorbs in others and again absorbs from others; but here in brine it is visible to the eye that even in one goose there is sixty against the brine; and even though nevertheless heaven forbid to rely on this — nevertheless here, where it is possible the issur was in the lower one and you will say it was the upper one, perhaps there is sixty, and it is also only chatichah hare'uyah lehischabed, and also for many poskim it is forbidden only as much as a peel — and if so, when that bird was roasted, each pot roast separately, it was nullified in sixty of the issur absorbed in its peel — it turns out the issur was already greatly weakened. Combined with this, there was already room to permit also the essence of the tereifot, for pure fat seals, except there was some doubt in this. From all these reasons I ruled that they should remove one of the five remaining geese, and those four should each cook in a pot alone and be served on separate tables in a manner that two do not come to one person's hand — end of his words — see there; and somewhat requires further study. ]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `Issur that fell with permitted, etc. In Orach Chayyim general rule 2 he wrote: if the meat remained in its soaking me'eit l'eit — certainly the vessel too is forbidden b'dieved and requires sixty against all of it if it is of wood or earthenware, for we hold kavush kemevushal even in vessels, and it is not similar to a libation-wine barrel that Semak permits to put beer in it l'chatchila, for with libation wine they were lenient; however of metal they do not require sixty against it b'dieved, only if it became forbidden through boiling; but l'chatchila it is permitted to soak in it again, for one does not soak the second meat me'eit l'eit — end of his words; and likewise further in general rule 30, and these are his words: since we say kavush kemevushal even in vessels, we say that in me'eit l'eit the vessel absorbed as with a libation-wine barrel that requires hag'alah; and likewise permitted soaked in a vessel of issur ben yomo; and likewise issur that remained in a vessel of permitted me'eit l'eit — all is forbidden — end of his words. And I cannot understand why permitted should be forbidden in a vessel of issur ben yomo — behold the issur is because of kavush after the measure of pickling, which is me'eit l'eit, when it becomes cooked; and behold at that hour it becomes not ben yomo, and the vessel has no power to forbid permitted soaked in it; and it is not similar to a libation-wine barrel that requires hag'alah even though it has much time — that is all twelve months, because its taste is for enhancement when putting wine (and beer) in it; but in truth other beverages are permitted to put in it, as is found in siman 137, and it is Talmud in the chapter "How does one redeem" — and from this I too wonder greatly at what he wrote above; and that which Semak permits to put beer in it l'chatchila — why did he need to write thus in the name of Semak, for plainly it is so in the chapter "How does one redeem," that R' Yehuda permitted R' Chiyya to put beer in it; and the reason is that they impair with wine, as written in siman 99 seif 7. And I wonder more: what did he challenge from putting beer — there it impairs the wine; and I saw in Darchei Moshe siman 69, and these are his words: since Orach Chayyim wrote regarding a vessel in which meat was soaked me'eit l'eit that the vessel became forbidden and it is permitted to return and soak meat in it, for one does not soak the second meat me'eit l'eit — it implies that if one soaks meat me'eit l'eit, the meat becomes forbidden from this — one should learn that kavush is considered literally cooked; and if one soaked an issur such as libation wine and the like in a vessel me'eit l'eit, it is forbidden to put permitted matter hot in it, and even cold there is issur if it is me'eit l'eit — end of his words; and I wondered at this, for behold we say in the Gemara I mentioned that it is permitted to put other beverages in it because they impair; and if permitted matter that he mentions refers to wine — difficult: why need to learn this law from Orach Chayyim — behold the mishnah is arranged: gentiles' flasks are forbidden; and on the essence of the law Orach Chayyim wrote, that sixty is required against all of it if it became forbidden through kavush me'eit l'eit — it is difficult for me, for Rashba wrote in Terumat HaDeshen that I brought in siman 99 seif 7, that the barrel that became forbidden through absorbing gentile wine when cold is only slight — and therefore a peel of the vessel suffices to declare fit; and likewise Tur siman 135 in the name of Rashba; and from there Rashba infers for other laws such as milk and blood regarding slight issur in a large vessel, as mentioned — from which it is not applicable to say regarding libation wine they were lenient, as Orach Chayyim's reasoning; and it turns out that in any case one should not say kavush kemevushal literally, for then a peel of the vessel would not suffice to declare it fit; and this is specifically in a gentile vessel that had wine and an Israelite wants to put kosher wine there — there is no impairment, even if there is much time — unlike other issurim, that after me'eit l'eit the forbidden vessel does not forbid even through cooking, for noten ta'am li'fegam; and why should the vessel be forbidden here through pickling issur into permitted afterward — at the hour it has power to forbid, meaning after me'eit l'eit, it is already noten ta'am li'fegam; and according to what appears there is no prohibition b'dieved of what fell or was cooked in a vessel in which issur was soaked me'eit l'eit, only l'chatchila as the law of) noten ta'am li'fegam; also what Orach Chayyim wrote to distinguish between wood and earthenware vessels and metal — I do not know this distinction, for we do not find anywhere that metal has more leniency; on the contrary, we find in siman 121 in Tur that there is no distinction between metal and wood and stone — only the essence is that in all vessels one should not forbid them b'dieved through issur falling in them me'eit l'eit, as appears to me.`,
    '1#ב': `And some are lenient in what is outside. And likewise in Tur Choshen Mishpat general rule 22.`,
    '1#ג': `And doubtful kavush. Meaning: we see it was kavush, only there is a doubt whether it was kavush a full day; and it appears he compares this to a doubt whether there was sixty against the issur that was spilled in siman 98 — except requires further study, for it is different there, for the issur is clear, only the spilling is doubtful — unlike here; and there is clear proof to permit from siman 201 seif 65 regarding a mikveh that was not presumed that its waters diminish — for even though sometimes they diminish, one need not return and immerse by law; and if so, a fortiori here: what there, where there is certainly impure — nevertheless we go to leniency because of the mikveh's presumption — a fortiori here, where there is only a good presumption to establish on presumption of kashrus, that initially we do not presume a defect from a doubt.`,
    '2#_': `And it is fitting to be concerned to be cautious, etc. And Rashal in the chapter Kol HaBasar siman 71 wrote, and these are his words: to rule the law — although a kli sheni does not cook, even if yad soledes bo in it, and also does not emit and absorb at once, such as forbidden matter already forbidden and permitted matter that came in a kli sheni — they are not forbidden one from another; but forbidden matter that fell into a kli sheni of permitted, or permitted into issur — it properly absorbs and forbids through all of it, since yad soledes bo in it like in a kli rishon, and all the more in iruy — end of his words. And so it appears to me to explain Tur's words, in what he challenged Rashba here — for I saw various explanations and my mind was not settled on what Tur wrote "heat into heat," for who mentions his name, except in this explanation of Tur, who inferred from Rashba's wording that he wrote "but any heat by fire emits," even if the yad does not soledet bo in it — for he included in kli sheni all its heat in one category to be stringent; if so, as the more severe law in it — namely hot into hot — so should the lighter law be — namely the yad does not soledet bo in it — and in all of them it should be forbidden through all of it; and with this he wrote there is no proof from heat of the slaughterhouse, for there it forbids only as much as a peel — rather necessarily that which Rashba said, that all laws of kli sheni are equal — meaning he holds also in hot into hot, which is more severe, a peel suffices; and on this he directed and said that also when the yad does not soledet bo in it, there is this issur; and on this he said he saw some authors write in kli sheni that the law is thus; and with this Tur's wonder on Beit Yosef and on this is resolved; and Tur said: in truth this is not in kli sheni, even cold into hot, only that the yad soledes bo in it — forbidden in all of them, and all the more hot into hot; and its law is regarding emitting from what is inside to absorb into another, like a kli rishon — even though regarding cooking there is a distinction between kli rishon and kli sheni, as written in Orach Chayyim siman 318 — nevertheless regarding absorbing and emitting there is no distinction; if so, there is a distinction in kli sheni, and Rashba should not have included all his laws in one rule, as appears to me — explanation of his words; and from this emerges that even in a kli sheni in which the yad soledes bo, it forbids all of it, as written in the name of Rashal, and likewise Beit Yosef — Tur's words in conclusion, as I wrote; and if it is hot so the hand is burned in it — then it forbids like cooked — meaning even in a kli sheni; and according to this, all the stringencies mentioned in siman 94 regarding ben yomo apply also in kli sheni, and not as Rama wrote there at the beginning of the siman — see there Orach Chayyim: if the spoon is ben yomo, meaning it was used within me'eit l'eit in a kli rishon. And for practical law one should rule stringently like Rashal, especially since Tur also holds thus — only in a place of great loss and an important matter one may rely on Beit Yosef in siman 68 and Rama in seif 3 here to be lenient b'dieved in kli sheni that it does not emit and absorb at all, even if the yad soledes bo in it; and if the yad does not soledet bo in it, one needs only rinsing, as appears to me; and see further what I wrote in siman 94 seif 14 in the name of Rashal, that in a thick item such as a hot piece of meat, its law is literally like a kli rishon — and what I wrote on it there.`,
    '3#א': `And if one is cold, etc. — the same law applies in basar b'chalav — thus it is written in Orach Chayyim general rule 29; and Rashal wrote in the chapter Kol HaBasar siman 74: it appears to me, if the issur is hot and the permitted cold and they rest one beside the other — it forbids all of it like salting; and if the permitted is hot and the issur cold and they rest one beside the other — a peel is required; and it appears that where there is not great loss one should be stringent thus, for even in salting Rama ruled thus in this siman seif 10.`,
    '3#ב': `Forbidden to pour from a vessel, etc. — this is the reason in Orach Chayyim, that tattei gavar, and he connects it in a stream; and Darchei Moshe wrote that one who pours fatty issur hot into fatty permitted cold forbids, because from the heat all of it melts; and he proved this from the law of a mouse that fell into fatty substance mentioned above siman 104, that it is forbidden even if the mouse is found at the bottom of the vessel.`,
    '4#א': `It forbids only as much as removal. Shach does not distinguish between hot through cooking or through roasting — and in both it forbids all of it; and Rashal wrote in the chapter Kol HaBasar siman 70 that this is the main point; and even according to Tur, who distinguishes here, Rashal wrote there that it is fitting for us to be stringent, since he concedes that in fatty substance it spreads through all of it, and we are not expert which fatty substance — perhaps it is fatty inside and not as Tur Choshen Mishpat copied to be lenient like Tur's words — end; and here in the hagahah nearby Rama himself ruled to be stringent.`,
    '4#ב': `That he did not cover it, it does not forbid, etc. That which is difficult from this — see what I wrote in siman 92 seif 2 there.`,
    '4#ג': `For Rashi, even part of it. It is a scribal error and must read for R' Yehuda, even part of it, as explained in siman 92; and likewise in Tur there is also a scribal error here; and likewise emended in Prisha; and likewise in Darchei Moshe.`,
    '5#א': `And even if the issur piece is lean, etc. Beit Yosef wrote that this is learned from what we find thus in siman 108 regarding reicha miltha — that even permitted fatty substance is forbidden for one who holds reicha miltha — and the reason is that permitted goes and imparts taste to the issur and returns and gives scent to the permitted. And I wondered: behold Maharai wrote in Hagahat ShaDaL on the law of reicha miltha, and these are his words: and one must distinguish between this and that which we say that what became forbidden cannot forbid, except where the issur itself can go there — for this too, the lean nevelah has no imparting of scent, for scent is different, because imparting carries the scent and the lean taste with it — end of his words; learn from this that the essence of this stringency is because of scent — for there we do not care about that view that what became forbidden cannot forbid; and since we hold reicha miltha in siman 108, b'dieved it is permitted — why should it be forbidden here even b'dieved, for there is here only scent issur — for this view that what became forbidden cannot forbid was said even when they touch one another — and nevertheless Maharai wrote we do not say thus except for reicha miltha specifically; and this is simple, that Tur did not intend here to forbid because of scent, for likewise in salting it is so, and scent does not apply; and Beit Yosef further challenged at the end of this siman from what Tur wrote there that what became forbidden cannot forbid, etc., and even if the permitted is fatty, etc., it is permitted; and Darchei Moshe wrote that here it deals with lean milk in the issur piece, that he calls it lean since it does not spread; and specifically in this we say the fatty substance spreads, since in any case there is milk on it; and it is difficult: behold we derive reicha miltha from it, and there it deals with lean meat, not lean milk; and likewise challenged in Drisha on it. And further difficult: behold milk was not mentioned here, only an issur piece. And I saw they reconcile: here, since the issur is forbidden from itself, we say even if the issur is lean and the permitted is fatty that carries the issur with it — since necessarily it emits nevelah gravy — therefore the fatty substance of the permitted mixes with nevelah gravy; but below, where it deals with absorbed issur, we say even though that piece that received the issur is fatty, the fatty substance in it does not carry the absorption of issur with it — and these words are entirely incorrect, for both have a boundary and limit: when the issur is from itself and it is lean, it has power to forbid a piece beside it without gravy up to as much as removal, and afterward its power ceases; and in absorbed issur it has power to go to the end of that piece itself where the issur fell, and from there onward it has no power at all without gravy, even though it rests directly on it — if so, a fortiori: what for absorbed issur, where in the very place of the issur there is fatty substance — nevertheless it does not help to give power to the absorbed issur to go outside its boundary — all the more for issur from itself, whose boundary to forbid is up to as much as a peel in the permitted piece, and no more — that the fatty substance of the permitted piece should not help take it outside its boundary; thus is it clear to me outside the boundary of another — the peel in issur from itself, as outside the boundary in the entire piece beside a piece forbidden through absorption of issur. And further clear proof from the source of this law, learned from what Tosafot and Rosh wrote in the chapter Gid HaNasheh, whom Beit Yosef brought in this siman — namely from what they wrote, that if a piece is found half in brine and half outside the brine, that which is outside the brine is not forbidden from what is below, and even if there is fatty substance in the brine we do not say it became nevelah and spreads upward, since the brine itself does not spread — and likewise Tur siman 69 and here at the end of the siman; and behold before you that brine is forbidden from itself, being blood that emerges from the meat — and even so fatty substance does not harm it to carry it where it goes; and what Beit Yosef wrote to reconcile, that blood is slick — is wondrous: just as there fatty substance does not cause blood, what is not its nature — so too it does not cause to absorb what is not in its nature to absorb anyway. Rather certainly this matter has no foundation, and the essence of the general rule — they said thus without examination; and Tur's words are wondrous, as Beit Yosef wrote; and were I not afraid, I would emend Tur's words in this language — except: even an issur piece that is fatty and a permitted piece that is lean, etc. — and this is what he says: not only in one piece that has milk in it — namely like that kid they roasted in its milk mentioned above — rather even if there are two pieces, one issur and one permitted, and that of issur is fatty and spreads the issur through all the permitted — and this is proven: if according to the text before us it is difficult — he should have said: not only in fatty issur and lean permitted, rather even in fatty permitted; also the wording "spreads through all of it" written in Tur is not resolved according to the text before us — he should have said that it spreads in the issur and returns and spreads in the permitted; and according to what I wrote, everything is resolved; and see from this what I will write in this siman seif 9 — Rashal's conclusion on this for practical law; and Rashal wrote in the chapter Gid HaNasheh siman 45: a piece that has blood of itself is also called a piece forbidden through absorption, and not issur from itself.`,
    '5#ב': `Requires netilat makom. For in those places the issur is considered more as if it is in substance; and we learn from Pesach — and see what I wrote in siman 94 seif 7.`,
    '5#ג': `That fatty substance applies to it. He intended this for nevelah brine, as Shach wrote in Sha'ar 37, that sixty is required against it even in salting; and Darchei Moshe wrote that nevelah brine or the like also spreads, similar to milk — for if not, he would certainly not disagree with the Gemara in Atemahata that they salted the forbidden gid and required only a peel.`,
    '7#א': `Or basar b'chalav. Rashal challenged in chapter 3 siman 45: behold we hold absorbed issur does not pass without gravy, even in basar b'chalav; and proof from a drop of milk that fell on meat in siman 92, that when that piece was forbidden without stirring and covering, it does not forbid others that touched hot one another — only Tur was not precise here, unless we explain what is written here in basar b'chalav deals with hot meat that touched cheese — that is considered issur from itself — end of his words; meaning the meat should be forbidden because of this also in Terumat HaDeshen HaArukh house 4 gate 4 likewise explicitly on meat forbidden from milk, that it does not return and forbid other meat without gravy, even if both are hot; and likewise further on daf 99 side 2, that milk or blood do not spread.`,
    '7#ב': `Even if they were roasted together. The reason in Tur is that the issur absorbed in the piece does not emerge from it to absorb in another without gravy — end of his explanation: it does not forbid at all, even as much as a peel; and likewise explicitly Rashal in Piskei HaGedolah siman 45, that a peel is not required.`,
    '7#ג': `But if it absorbed from spreading things, etc. — it implies milk does not spread, for if not, why do we say in siman 92 seif 2 that that piece alone was forbidden and not the others — and as I wrote nearby also in the name of Terumat HaDeshen HaArukh and Rashal; and above siman 87 seif 10 proves we say milk is called issur that spreads, as written there; and see what I wrote in seif 9, what is difficult on Shulchan Aruch's ruling here.`,
    '7#ד': `But a vessel that absorbed, etc. — the reason for this is that specifically food that has something to emit from its body — then absorption does not pass without gravy; but a vessel that has no emission from its body — then what it absorbed emerges properly; and according to this it is simple that a vessel is not forbidden from food with absorbed issur that does not spread, such as liver or meat salted in an unperforated vessel — as will be explained in siman 108 seif 1, that this does not spread.`,
    '8#א': `And some forbid. The reason is that we do not rely on this answer in the Gemara; and also according to this answer one would need to check whether the membrane was not torn — likewise Maharai in the name of Semak.`,
    '8#ב': `And likewise in the membrane on the gid. Likewise Maharai; and Darchei Moshe learned from this wording that if one cooked the gid with the membrane on it without the forbidden substance, sixty is required against the entire piece; and above siman 64 seif 2 Rama wrote regarding salting that in all membranes one needs only a peel in a place of slight loss.`,
    '9#א': `And if it is the fat of the gid. According to his reason, as he wrote in seif 5 that lean milk does not spread and does not require sixty to nullify it.`,
    '9#ב': `And if the meat that was salted, etc. That it adheres to the milk, as he concludes afterward — and all this, etc.`,
    '9#ג': `If there is not in each one of them, etc. Meaning: if milk touched each one of them and there is not in each one sixty against the milk — forbidden, lest it absorbed much and has no partnership to nullify.`,
    '9#ד': `That milk does not spread from piece to piece. From this is also inferred a leniency — namely, if it is clear milk touched only one of them, then the others are permitted; and I wonder at Shulchan Aruch, whose rulings contradict one another: above seif 7 he ruled that if it absorbed from spreading things, such as fatty substance, it forbids its fellow even without gravy — and that is Rashba's view whom Tur brought; and here he ruled that milk absorption does not emerge without gravy — and that is Maharam's view in Mordechai chapter 3; and Beit Yosef brings it and says: here it deals with lean milk that does not spread, for in Mordechai it is written explicitly that this law is on the milk on the stomach, which is the main milk; and further, if the milk were lean, why would each need sixty by itself — therefore one must say Maharam disputes Rashba and holds that even in fatty issur it does not emerge without gravy, whatever is absorbed in it — end; wonder on Shulchan Aruch that he did not notice this dispute; also in Levush it is written like Shulchan Aruch here — and it is very wondrous; and one should not say that above it deals with roasting and here with salting — for we conclude in the Gemara salted is like roasted — and Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch's conclusion is thus, that it is like roasted.`,
    '9#ה': `Even if they are pieces fit to be served to guests. The reason is that the milk in them is absorbed issur, and absorbed issur cannot make a piece fit to be served to guests, etc. — as written siman 101.`,
    '9#ו': `And some say that all salting does not forbid, etc. Meaning: even fatty substance — and it is Ravyah's view in Beit Yosef, because the issur does not pass through all but remains in its place; and Rama says that although it is fitting for us to be stringent and require sixty to satisfy those who hold that even in salting the issur spreads through all of it — that is in a fatty issur; and to be lenient in what is not fatty, but we are not expert, etc.`,
    '9#ז': `And even though there is in this some leniency if the issur is fatty. Explanation: regarding whether there is milk in that piece — for according to those who hold sixty is required, meaning sixty is required in that piece itself against the milk in it, and other pieces do not combine) to nullify milk in that piece; and according to custom we combine also other pieces; and further consider if the milk is not attached to any piece, only rests beside one of them — we should have said they do not combine, and in each one it touched, sixty is required by itself; nevertheless we hold all combine; and in Levush the explanation of the above leniency is regarding that we do not require a peel — for according to those who say salting forbids as much as a peel, the peel nevertheless becomes forbidden, etc.; and he was not precise here, for in truth even according to custom a peel is required where it touched — whereas where it did not touch, certainly nothing is needed according to all, for it forbids only the peel that touched it.`,
    '9#ח': `Such as milk or brine. One must say blood is also included in this, even though blood does not spread, as is found in many places — nevertheless sometimes there are mixtures of brine and fatty substance; therefore we find in siman 70 that salting forbids in blood in sixty. And I will write Rashal's conclusion in the chapter Gid HaNasheh siman 28: that which we find a distinction between cooking and salting and roasting and baking — that is specifically for R' Eliezer of Metz and those who follow him, who hold in these three one removes only the place of spreading and according to estimation; but we hold like R' Avraham of Sens in the name of R' Yehuda HaLavan and Maharai after them, that the law of these three is like cooking and requires sixty like other issurim; and automatically chametz on Pesach in any amount; and nevertheless we do not say in these three they are like cooking, only stringently — meaning sixty is required against the milk, and if it is attached, sixty against the entire piece; but not to be lenient — meaning not to require only as much as removal even when there is sixty — nevertheless as much as removal is required; therefore he ruled regarding matzah that folded somewhat in the oven before Pesach, sixty is required in that matzah against the issur and as much as removal, as Maharai wrote in Terumat HaDeshen — and not like Rama in Orach Chayyim, who did not require sixty in matzah. Further Rashal concludes in his Orach Chayyim and in his book that we do not hold as Tur wrote, that also in salting, and permitted fatty and issur lean — all is forbidden; for also in roasting this is a novelty, that we say permitted imparts taste to issur and makes it fatty, and a colleague does not do so — nevertheless in salting we certainly are not so stringent, only we say: wherever the issur cannot go and spread, even the fatty piece is permitted.`,
    '11#_': `And some dispute and say that in salting there is no distinction, etc. The reason is that in salting there is no power in what is bland to nullify the power of salting; and likewise salting has no power to make the bland salty, for such is the way of salt — it cannot be nullified except through rinsing — likewise Semak; and in Tur it is written in this plain meaning, and these are his words: it appears he holds that heat through salting forbids all of it, etc.; and Beit Yosef challenged: behold Rashba himself, in what deals specifically with fatty substance, etc. — and every examiner must search with all his power to justify our rabbi Tur's words from this wondrous difficulty, that he copied the beginning of the words and not the end; and I saw emendations on this that do not please me at all; and what appears to my mind in this: Tur inferred from Rashba's words, who distinguished between issur salted above or below — from which the reason of the matter that all is forbidden in fatty issur is because the permitted itself became entirely hot, and through this it receives taste of issur that is fatty; but Tur does not explain thus, rather even though the permitted is cold — nevertheless the fatty substance that comes from salted issur goes and spreads in the permitted, even though it is cold, because spreading itself is hot; and the difference between these two reasons: if according to Rashba's reason, that it is hot — it implies that in one piece that absorbs d'oraisa issur through salting, the entire piece becomes forbidden, since salting of fatty issur makes the permitted piece hot — likewise in lean in one piece, for they did not require fatty substance except from piece to piece, which does not absorb in lean; but in one piece it certainly emits what it absorbed in all its parts, since it is hot; and on this he says: it appears "heat through salting" — for at first glance it is superfluous language, this "heat" — he should have said that through salting it forbids all of it; rather he intended what I wrote, that it implies from Rashba's words that the heat of salting causes the entire piece to boil and automatically the entire piece is forbidden, even in lean; and Rosh could not have written thus, only in lean there is issur only as much as a peel, even in one piece; and therefore Tur afterward wrote properly; and also what he distinguished, etc. — meaning: and further, why did he distinguish between above and below — from another side thus appears to my mind in this explanation; and even if you find somewhere in Rashba's words that it does not seem to him as our rabbi Tur explains his words here — nevertheless our rabbi is saved from the great difficulty we mentioned, that he did not examine Rashba, only the beginning of his words and not the end, which contradict one another.`,
    '12#_': `And likewise if it was salted, etc. In Beit Yosef in the name of responsum of Rashba, that it is permitted to salt meat in a vessel into which gentiles put wine for preservation, and he gave many reasons: one, that this does not emit issur; and further, that it did not absorb except something entangled like oil or brine, etc. — end of his words; and in siman 70 seif 2 I wrote that Rashal disagrees and it appears to him that salting does not cause a vessel to emit at all, even d'oraisa issur absorbed in a vessel.`,
    '13#_': `And one who is stringent l'chatchila, etc. See what I wrote in siman 95 seif 6 on this.`,
    '14#_': `Even though the salt is noten ta'am li'fegam. See what I wrote in siman 98 seif 4 on the law of acting according to its taste, and what Tur wrote here at the end of the siman: one must estimate as if all the blood were salted — meaning that sixty does not help as long as the taste is sensed; and on this they said that even in a thousand it is not nullified, because of nosen ta'am — meaning any amount that gives taste, as opposed to initial intent; and likewise Rama wrote at the end of siman 98.`,
  },
};
